<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rurik Rostislavich</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1212</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rurik Rostislavich was a prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurikids</a> who held the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> several times between 1173 and 1210. In 1203, allied with the Olgovichi and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>, he sacked Kiev during his feud with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-the-great">Roman the Great</a>, who later forced him to become a monk. He returned to power after Roman's death in 1205 and ended his life as prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rurik, whose baptismal name was Basil, was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostislav-i-of-kiev">Rostislav I of Kiev</a>. In the 1160s he ruled the land of the Drevlians, and in 1168 he became prince of Ovruch. He held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliky-novgorod">Novgorod the Great</a> in 1170 and 1171 and was prince of Belgorod from 1173 to 1194, while the succession conflicts of the princely house brought him briefly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> in 1173 and again in 1180 and 1181. He was married to Anna, and among their children was Rostislav II of Kiev.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The sources disagree about the years after 1181. The Kievan Chronicle describes Rurik as co-ruler with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-vsevolodovich">Sviatoslav Vsevolodovich</a>, prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> since 1177, in a partnership that lasted until Sviatoslav's death in 1194. The Novgorod Fourth Chronicle and the Sofia First Chronicle, in their entries for 1180 and 1185, have Sviatoslav reigning alone and do not name Rurik as co-prince. After 1194 Rurik ruled Kiev in his own right until a conflict with the Olgovichi and with Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-the-great">Roman Mstislavich</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> cost him the throne.</p><p>Having lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, Rurik sought an alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>. After a short stay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a>, where he built the Church of St. Paraskebas, he attacked Kiev with his kinsmen and a Cuman army and sacked the city in 1203. He was confined to a monastery in 1204, but after Roman's death in 1205 he abandoned his vows and took the throne again.</p><p>His cousin Vsevolod Chermny held that Rurik's monastic vows made his rule invalid and seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> from him more than once. In 1210 Rurik was forced to give up the Kievan throne to Vsevolod, and he governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a> for the rest of his life, until his death on 19 April 1212.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rurik Rostislavich” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The armies of Andrei Bogolyubsky sacked it in 1169, and <b>Rurik Rostislavich</b> and his Cuman allies sacked it again in 1203.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-kiev">Principality of Kiev</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernihiv</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.49,31.3&amp;year=1202" aria-label="Chernihiv on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Rurik Rostislavich"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5069 5724h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5069 5724h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=51.49,31.3&amp;year=1202">What was here in 1202? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
